WEBVTT

00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:02.431
Here's that steel ball, going round in a

00:00:02.532 --> 00:00:04.220
horizontal circle on a string.

00:00:05.043 --> 00:00:07.674
The string slants, tracing out a cone, so

00:00:07.695 --> 00:00:09.642
this is called a conical pendulum.

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:13.096
Now imagine the ball going faster and faster.

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:15.323
Could it ever go fast enough for the

00:00:15.364 --> 00:00:16.908
string to be perfectly horizontal?

00:00:17.635 --> 00:00:18.264
Make a prediction.

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:23.869
Only two forces act on the ball.

00:00:24.311 --> 00:00:26.495
The tension pulls along the string, towards the

00:00:26.516 --> 00:00:28.901
support, and the weight pulls straight down.

00:00:29.544 --> 00:00:32.230
There's no third arrow for centripetal force, because

00:00:32.251 --> 00:00:33.553
that's a job, not a force.

00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:35.924
And notice where the centre of the circle

00:00:35.945 --> 00:00:36.004
is.

00:00:36.588 --> 00:00:37.554
It isn't the support.

00:00:38.078 --> 00:00:40.244
It's level with the ball, directly below the

00:00:40.285 --> 00:00:40.605
support.

00:00:41.369 --> 00:00:43.240
So the net force has to be horizontal,

00:00:43.642 --> 00:00:44.909
pointing at that centre.

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:46.433
Let's name the parts.

00:00:47.198 --> 00:00:49.389
The string has length L, and it makes

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.138
an angle theta with the vertical.

00:00:51.861 --> 00:00:53.782
The radius of the circle is the horizontal

00:00:53.863 --> 00:00:56.364
distance from the ball to the centre, so

00:00:56.705 --> 00:00:58.266
r equals L sine theta.

00:00:59.188 --> 00:01:00.711
And the height of the support above the

00:01:00.752 --> 00:01:03.519
circle is h, which is L cosine theta.

00:01:04.261 --> 00:01:06.421
Keep those straight, because r is not L,

00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:08.563
and mixing them up is the most common

00:01:08.604 --> 00:01:09.584
slip in these questions.

00:01:10.249 --> 00:01:12.765
Now split the tension into two parts.

00:01:13.389 --> 00:01:16.677
Its vertical part is F T cosine theta,

00:01:17.139 --> 00:01:19.204
and its horizontal part, pointing at the centre,

00:01:19.606 --> 00:01:21.330
is F T sine theta.

00:01:22.032 --> 00:01:23.956
The ball doesn't move up or down, so

00:01:24.278 --> 00:01:26.261
the vertical part balances the weight.

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:29.722
F T cosine theta equals m g.

00:01:30.466 --> 00:01:32.894
The horizontal part has nothing to balance it,

00:01:33.256 --> 00:01:35.704
so it's the net force, doing the centripetal

00:01:35.745 --> 00:01:36.005
job.

00:01:36.769 --> 00:01:39.583
F T sine theta equals m v squared

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:40.226
over r.

00:01:41.011 --> 00:01:42.817
One string, doing two jobs at once.

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:44.634
That answers the question.

00:01:45.198 --> 00:01:47.122
If the string were horizontal, it would have

00:01:47.324 --> 00:01:49.909
no vertical part at all, and nothing would

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.333
hold the ball up against its weight.

00:01:51.976 --> 00:01:54.971
So the string always slants, however fast the

00:01:54.993 --> 00:01:55.454
ball goes.

00:01:56.519 --> 00:01:58.761
It also means the tension is always bigger

00:01:58.802 --> 00:02:00.763
than the weight, because only part of it

00:02:00.864 --> 00:02:01.805
is holding the ball up.

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:05.036
Divide the horizontal equation by the vertical one.

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:07.826
The tension and the mass both cancel, and

00:02:07.847 --> 00:02:10.516
you get tan theta equals v squared over

00:02:10.758 --> 00:02:11.038
r g.

00:02:11.742 --> 00:02:13.854
So a faster ball swings out to a

00:02:13.895 --> 00:02:14.498
bigger angle.

00:02:15.302 --> 00:02:17.488
And as theta gets closer to ninety degrees,

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:21.119
cosine theta shrinks, so the tension needed grows

00:02:21.321 --> 00:02:21.982
very large.

00:02:22.685 --> 00:02:24.912
That's why the string never quite reaches horizontal.

00:02:25.671 --> 00:02:26.622
Let's try an example.

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:30.251
A zero point two zero kilogram bob hangs

00:02:30.392 --> 00:02:32.534
on a zero point five zero metre string,

00:02:32.935 --> 00:02:34.416
at thirty degrees to the vertical.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.751
The bob is just the small mass on

00:02:36.792 --> 00:02:37.053
the end.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:38.584
First the radius.

00:02:39.611 --> 00:02:42.158
r is zero point five zero times sine

00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:44.667
thirty, which is zero point two five metres.

00:02:45.511 --> 00:02:47.518
Now the tension, from the vertical equation.

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:50.804
F T equals m g over cosine theta,

00:02:51.306 --> 00:02:53.928
so zero point two zero times nine point

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:55.670
eight, over cosine thirty.

00:02:56.293 --> 00:02:58.921
That's about two point two six newtons, a

00:02:58.942 --> 00:03:00.547
bit more than the weight of one point

00:03:00.628 --> 00:03:01.490
nine six newtons.

00:03:02.487 --> 00:03:03.278
Next, the speed.

00:03:03.902 --> 00:03:06.431
From tan theta equals v squared over r

00:03:06.491 --> 00:03:06.732
g,

00:03:07.293 --> 00:03:09.420
v equals the square root of r g

00:03:09.521 --> 00:03:10.283
tan theta.

00:03:10.945 --> 00:03:12.749
That's the square root of zero point two

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:15.894
five times nine point eight times tan thirty,

00:03:16.455 --> 00:03:18.519
about one point one nine metres per second.

00:03:19.261 --> 00:03:21.043
As a check, the horizontal part of the

00:03:21.104 --> 00:03:23.847
tension, two point two six times sine thirty,

00:03:24.249 --> 00:03:26.110
is about one point one three newtons,

00:03:26.632 --> 00:03:28.714
and m v squared over r gives the

00:03:28.755 --> 00:03:29.295
same value.

00:03:30.268 --> 00:03:30.818
And the period.

00:03:31.441 --> 00:03:33.102
The ball goes once round a circle of

00:03:33.182 --> 00:03:36.023
radius zero point two five metres at one

00:03:36.083 --> 00:03:38.765
point one nine metres per second, so T

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.646
equals two pi r over v, about one

00:03:41.686 --> 00:03:42.887
point three two seconds.

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:44.475
Here's a surprise.

00:03:45.018 --> 00:03:46.981
If you put the speed formula into T

00:03:47.122 --> 00:03:49.045
equals two pi r over v,

00:03:49.786 --> 00:03:51.709
r over tan theta turns out to be

00:03:51.749 --> 00:03:53.011
just the height h.

00:03:53.553 --> 00:03:53.753
So T

00:03:54.315 --> 00:03:56.520
equals two pi times the square root of

00:03:56.541 --> 00:03:56.560
h

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:57.563
over g.

00:03:58.226 --> 00:04:00.371
The period depends only on how far the

00:04:00.432 --> 00:04:02.116
circle sits below the support.

00:04:02.678 --> 00:04:04.055
The mass doesn't matter at all.

00:04:04.496 --> 00:04:06.728
The classic slip is using the string length

00:04:07.011 --> 00:04:07.654
as the radius.

00:04:08.258 --> 00:04:10.960
Put r equals zero point five zero into

00:04:10.981 --> 00:04:12.983
the speed formula, and you get about one

00:04:13.064 --> 00:04:15.206
point six eight metres per second, which is

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:16.027
far too fast.

00:04:16.749 --> 00:04:18.590
The ball goes round a circle of radius

00:04:18.871 --> 00:04:20.413
L sine theta, not L.

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:22.136
Here's a way to hold it together.

00:04:23.106 --> 00:04:24.558
One string, two jobs.

00:04:25.142 --> 00:04:28.132
The up part holds, the in part turns.

00:04:29.116 --> 00:04:30.720
That works for a light string and a

00:04:30.781 --> 00:04:32.826
ball going round a horizontal circle at a

00:04:32.907 --> 00:04:33.608
steady speed.

00:04:34.351 --> 00:04:34.952
If you get stuck,

00:04:35.495 --> 00:04:37.259
ask which part of the tension holds the

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.325
ball up, and which part turns it.

00:04:41.997 --> 00:04:42.400
Your turn.

00:04:42.945 --> 00:04:45.409
A zero point five zero kilogram bob on

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:47.794
a string of length zero point eight zero

00:04:47.855 --> 00:04:48.235
metres

00:04:48.737 --> 00:04:51.081
moves in a horizontal circle, with the string

00:04:51.142 --> 00:04:52.765
at thirty-five degrees to the vertical.

00:04:53.388 --> 00:04:55.975
Calculate the radius of the circle, the tension

00:04:55.996 --> 00:04:57.459
in the string, and the speed of the

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:57.720
bob.

00:04:58.324 --> 00:04:59.331
Pause here to answer.

00:05:09.935 --> 00:05:12.559
The radius is zero point eight zero times

00:05:12.741 --> 00:05:15.866
sine thirty-five, about zero point four five nine

00:05:15.927 --> 00:05:16.267
metres.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:20.154
The tension is m g over cosine thirty-five,

00:05:20.556 --> 00:05:22.859
which is four point nine zero over zero

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.283
point eight one nine, about five point nine

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:25.864
eight newtons.

00:05:26.526 --> 00:05:28.246
And v is the square root of r

00:05:28.588 --> 00:05:32.009
g tan thirty-five, about one point seven seven

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:32.730
metres per second.

00:05:33.714 --> 00:05:36.047
The slip is using zero point eight zero

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:36.831
as the radius.

00:05:38.511 --> 00:05:38.936
Last one.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:42.006
A conical pendulum has a string of length

00:05:42.187 --> 00:05:45.416
zero point four zero metres, at twenty-five degrees

00:05:45.457 --> 00:05:46.058
to the vertical.

00:05:46.644 --> 00:05:47.796
Calculate its period.

00:05:48.586 --> 00:05:49.742
Pause here to answer.

00:06:00.407 --> 00:06:03.350
The circle sits L cosine theta below the

00:06:03.391 --> 00:06:06.413
support, so h is zero point four zero

00:06:06.474 --> 00:06:09.997
times cosine twenty-five, about zero point three six

00:06:10.039 --> 00:06:10.618
three metres.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.668
Then T equals two pi times the square

00:06:13.709 --> 00:06:15.996
root of h over g, about one point

00:06:16.058 --> 00:06:16.860
two one seconds.

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:19.766
The slip is using the whole string length

00:06:19.887 --> 00:06:22.090
instead of h, which gives one point two

00:06:22.171 --> 00:06:22.792
seven seconds.

00:06:25.203 --> 00:06:25.343
So

00:06:25.886 --> 00:06:27.974
the slant of the string gives the tension

00:06:27.994 --> 00:06:29.561
a part that points inwards.

00:06:30.326 --> 00:06:31.543
A road can do the same trick.

00:06:32.145 --> 00:06:33.609
Tilt the road towards the centre of the

00:06:33.649 --> 00:06:36.054
bend, and the normal force, which is always

00:06:36.094 --> 00:06:38.059
at right angles to the road, tilts too.

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:40.158
Now part of it points inwards.

00:06:41.042 --> 00:06:43.228
So how does a banked road cut down

00:06:43.268 --> 00:06:44.732
the friction a car needs?

00:06:47.495 --> 00:06:48.317
Here are the key notes.

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:50.388
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:50.429 --> 00:06:50.850
to copy them.
